WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.556
Imagine Torrance must address an aging public facility.

2
00:00:03.556 --> 00:00:05.237
One proposal renovates it.

3
00:00:05.237 --> 00:00:07.629
Another replaces it on the same site.

4
00:00:07.629 --> 00:00:12.414
A third moves services into leased space and sells or reuses the property.

5
00:00:12.414 --> 00:00:14.677
Advocates quickly choose favorites.

6
00:00:14.677 --> 00:00:21.078
Yet each option solves a slightly different problem, on a different schedule, with different risks.

7
00:00:21.078 --> 00:00:28.384
Alternative analysis creates a fair comparison before momentum turns one concept into the only imaginable choice.

8
00:00:28.384 --> 00:00:39.323
This episode is educational preparation, not financial, legal, engineering, environmental, appraisal, procurement, real estate, investment, or municipal advisory advice.

9
00:00:39.323 --> 00:00:46.379
Real alternatives require current technical studies, public engagement, legal review, and qualified analysis.

10
00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:48.580
Begin with the decision statement.

11
00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:51.622
Describe the need without embedding a solution.

12
00:00:51.622 --> 00:00:59.519
Instead of saying, build a new center, say, provide safe, accessible service capacity for a defined population and period.

13
00:00:59.519 --> 00:01:03.144
State required outcomes, constraints, and decision date.

14
00:01:03.144 --> 00:01:04.827
Always include a baseline.

15
00:01:04.827 --> 00:01:10.523
The baseline may be maintain current service, repair only, defer, or take no new action.

16
00:01:10.523 --> 00:01:12.400
No action is rarely costless.

17
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:18.938
It may include failures, emergency work, inefficiency, lost service, liability, or foregone benefits.

18
00:01:18.938 --> 00:01:20.297
Describe it honestly.

19
00:01:20.297 --> 00:01:22.757
Screen for feasibility before ranking.

20
00:01:22.757 --> 00:01:24.634
Does the city have authority?

21
00:01:24.634 --> 00:01:26.058
Is the site available?

22
00:01:26.058 --> 00:01:31.431
Can the option meet safety, accessibility, environmental, and service requirements?

23
00:01:31.431 --> 00:01:33.243
Can it be delivered in time?

24
00:01:33.243 --> 00:01:37.839
An infeasible option should not earn points simply because it is cheap.

25
00:01:37.839 --> 00:01:40.752
For feasible options, use common assumptions.

26
00:01:40.752 --> 00:01:50.267
Apply the same study period, inflation framework, service demand, labor assumptions, discounting approach if used, and treatment of residual value.

27
00:01:50.267 --> 00:01:51.432
Record exceptions.

28
00:01:51.432 --> 00:01:53.633
Compare life-cycle fiscal effects.

29
00:01:53.633 --> 00:02:02.177
Include planning, land, design, construction, financing, operation, maintenance, renewal, transition, decommissioning, and disposal.

30
00:02:02.177 --> 00:02:03.989
Show costs by fund and year.

31
00:02:03.989 --> 00:02:10.980
Identify revenues or savings, but test whether they are incremental, collectible, unrestricted, and durable.

32
00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:12.793
Money is only one dimension.

33
00:02:12.793 --> 00:02:24.444
Add service quality, equity, accessibility, safety, climate resilience, environmental effects, neighborhood impact, deliverability, flexibility, and consistency with adopted plans.

34
00:02:24.444 --> 00:02:26.839
Define each criterion before scoring.

35
00:02:26.839 --> 00:02:28.328
Weights express values.

36
00:02:28.328 --> 00:02:35.318
If fiscal cost receives forty percent and equity receives five percent, the matrix has made a policy choice.

37
00:02:35.318 --> 00:02:39.785
Make weights public and test whether modest changes alter the result.

38
00:02:39.785 --> 00:02:42.439
Do not let false precision hide judgment.

39
00:02:42.439 --> 00:02:45.481
Use both quantitative and qualitative evidence.

40
00:02:45.481 --> 00:02:48.135
A dollar estimate can still be uncertain.

41
00:02:48.135 --> 00:02:53.702
A qualitative rating can still be disciplined if the standard and evidence are stated.

42
00:02:53.702 --> 00:02:55.902
Use ranges and confidence ratings.

43
00:02:55.902 --> 00:03:01.016
High, medium, and low confidence may be more honest than another decimal place.

44
00:03:01.016 --> 00:03:02.958
Risk should not be a footnote.

45
00:03:02.958 --> 00:03:04.770
Create a risk-adjusted view.

46
00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:12.279
For each alternative, identify major uncertainty, likelihood, consequence, mitigation, owner, and residual exposure.

47
00:03:12.279 --> 00:03:20.758
Consider schedule delay, escalation, site condition, permitting, demand, revenue, operations, technology, and community acceptance.

48
00:03:20.758 --> 00:03:23.606
Options also have value through flexibility.

49
00:03:23.606 --> 00:03:26.066
A phased project can preserve choices.

50
00:03:26.066 --> 00:03:28.267
A modular design can expand later.

51
00:03:28.267 --> 00:03:32.733
A lease can reduce upfront cost but expose the city to renewal terms.

52
00:03:32.733 --> 00:03:36.682
Ownership can provide control while concentrating asset risk.

53
00:03:36.682 --> 00:03:40.695
Identify decisions that can be reversed and those that cannot.

54
00:03:40.695 --> 00:03:43.873
Apply this to the hypothetical Torrance facility.

55
00:03:43.873 --> 00:03:45.560
Define three alternatives.

56
00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:49.841
Alternative one renovates in place and uses temporary swing space.

57
00:03:49.841 --> 00:03:55.483
Alternative two builds replacement space, then removes or repurposes the old structure.

58
00:03:55.483 --> 00:04:00.413
Alternative three leases existing private space and adapts service delivery.

59
00:04:00.413 --> 00:04:02.424
First compare service outcomes.

60
00:04:02.424 --> 00:04:09.234
Can each provide required rooms, public access, security, parking or transit access, and future capacity?

61
00:04:09.234 --> 00:04:11.115
Next compare fiscal profiles.

62
00:04:11.115 --> 00:04:15.915
Renovation may have lower initial cost but greater unknown-condition risk.

63
00:04:15.915 --> 00:04:20.650
Replacement may have higher capital cost but lower near-term maintenance.

64
00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:25.514
Leasing may shift capital needs into recurring payments and reduce control.

65
00:04:25.514 --> 00:04:27.979
These are hypotheses, not conclusions.

66
00:04:27.979 --> 00:04:29.017
Obtain evidence.

67
00:04:29.017 --> 00:04:30.574
Then compare transition.

68
00:04:30.574 --> 00:04:33.103
Renovation may disrupt current service.

69
00:04:33.103 --> 00:04:35.827
Replacement may require a longer schedule.

70
00:04:35.827 --> 00:04:39.395
Leasing may move users and require tenant improvements.

71
00:04:39.395 --> 00:04:43.546
Include moving, downtime, communication, and parallel operation.

72
00:04:43.546 --> 00:04:45.103
Run break-even analysis.

73
00:04:45.103 --> 00:04:50.486
Ask how high renovation change orders must become before replacement is preferable.

74
00:04:50.486 --> 00:04:55.610
Ask what lease escalation or occupancy period changes the ownership comparison.

75
00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:58.399
Ask what residual value assumptions matter.

76
00:04:58.399 --> 00:05:03.069
Show the threshold rather than pretending to predict one future exactly.

77
00:05:03.069 --> 00:05:05.015
Conduct a distribution review.

78
00:05:05.015 --> 00:05:07.091
Which neighborhoods gain access?

79
00:05:07.091 --> 00:05:08.388
Who travels farther?

80
00:05:08.388 --> 00:05:10.269
Who experiences construction?

81
00:05:10.269 --> 00:05:12.345
Which users need accommodations?

82
00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:16.885
Does a lower-cost option transfer burden to people with fewer choices?

83
00:05:16.885 --> 00:05:18.636
Finally, hold a pre-mortem.

84
00:05:18.636 --> 00:05:22.074
Imagine each option failed five years after approval.

85
00:05:22.074 --> 00:05:23.630
What caused the failure?

86
00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:29.273
This exercise can expose neglected operating, procurement, governance, or demand risks.

87
00:05:29.273 --> 00:05:31.544
Sequence can be an alternative too.

88
00:05:31.544 --> 00:05:40.170
The city might first complete urgent safety work, collect condition and usage data, and reserve a later decision on full replacement.

89
00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:46.916
Staging can reduce uncertainty, but it can also create duplication, escalation, or prolonged disruption.

90
00:05:46.916 --> 00:05:48.213
Price those effects.

91
00:05:48.213 --> 00:05:49.445
Check dependencies.

92
00:05:49.445 --> 00:05:58.850
One option may require a utility relocation, property acquisition, grant deadline, partner agreement, temporary site, or related capital project.

93
00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:03.326
Put dependencies on a schedule and identify the consequence of delay.

94
00:06:03.326 --> 00:06:07.801
A low estimate with an unrealistic schedule is not a low-cost option.

95
00:06:07.801 --> 00:06:09.358
Examine procurement fit.

96
00:06:09.358 --> 00:06:18.763
A conventional design-bid-build process, design-build approach where authorized, lease, or service contract can change control, timing, and risk.

97
00:06:18.763 --> 00:06:23.563
Delivery method should follow project characteristics and legal authority.

98
00:06:23.563 --> 00:06:28.427
It should not be selected merely because its early estimate appears faster.

99
00:06:28.427 --> 00:06:31.346
Include carbon and resilience where relevant.

100
00:06:31.346 --> 00:06:34.265
A renovation may preserve embodied materials.

101
00:06:34.265 --> 00:06:38.351
A replacement may improve energy performance and accessibility.

102
00:06:38.351 --> 00:06:42.178
A leased location may shift exposure rather than reduce it.

103
00:06:42.178 --> 00:06:46.718
Describe physical risk, operating effect, and who pays for adaptation.

104
00:06:46.718 --> 00:06:50.156
Before final scoring, conduct an assumption workshop.

105
00:06:50.156 --> 00:06:58.069
Ask finance, operations, engineering, users, accessibility specialists, and community representatives to challenge inputs.

106
00:06:58.069 --> 00:07:01.247
Record disagreement instead of averaging it away.

107
00:07:01.247 --> 00:07:04.296
A visible range is better than false consensus.

108
00:07:04.296 --> 00:07:06.689
Present results as a decision record.

109
00:07:06.689 --> 00:07:16.842
Include why alternatives were selected, assumptions, evidence, exclusions, scoring, sensitivity, public input, unresolved questions, and approval conditions.

110
00:07:16.842 --> 00:07:20.851
Preserve the record so future reviewers understand the choice.

111
00:07:20.851 --> 00:07:23.762
Do not use the matrix to manufacture consent.

112
00:07:23.762 --> 00:07:31.393
Decision makers may select an option that does not have the highest composite score if they explain the policy reason.

113
00:07:31.393 --> 00:07:34.950
Transparency is more important than mechanical ranking.

114
00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:38.701
Your learner artifact is an Alternatives Comparison Brief.

115
00:07:38.701 --> 00:07:40.576
It has six pages or sections.

116
00:07:40.576 --> 00:07:44.844
One: need statement, required outcomes, constraints, and baseline.

117
00:07:44.844 --> 00:07:48.595
Two: descriptions of at least three feasible alternatives.

118
00:07:48.595 --> 00:07:52.281
Three: common assumptions and life-cycle fiscal profiles.

119
00:07:52.281 --> 00:07:56.032
Four: criteria, weights, evidence, confidence, and scores.

120
00:07:56.032 --> 00:08:00.301
Five: risks, break-even thresholds, distribution, and sensitivity.

121
00:08:00.301 --> 00:08:04.892
Six: recommendation, conditions, rejected options, and monitoring plan.

122
00:08:04.892 --> 00:08:08.966
Use only sourced Torrance facts if you apply it to a real item.

123
00:08:08.966 --> 00:08:12.200
Mark all illustrative assumptions as hypothetical.

124
00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:14.011
Now, three recall questions.

125
00:08:14.011 --> 00:08:18.408
Question one: Why should the need statement avoid naming a solution?

126
00:08:18.408 --> 00:08:26.492
Answer: Naming a solution too early narrows the analysis and can prevent genuinely different ways of meeting the public need.

127
00:08:26.492 --> 00:08:29.855
Question two: What does sensitivity analysis reveal?

128
00:08:29.855 --> 00:08:36.063
Answer: It shows which assumptions, weights, or thresholds can change the preferred alternative.

129
00:08:36.063 --> 00:08:39.556
Question three: Why is no action not necessarily free?

130
00:08:39.556 --> 00:08:46.734
Answer: Existing conditions can create maintenance, failure, service, safety, liability, and opportunity costs.

131
00:08:46.734 --> 00:08:57.274
This episode was informed by the public nine-module outline for the Lincoln Institute of Land Policy's Foundations of Local Government Finance in the United States.

132
00:08:57.274 --> 00:09:02.030
No account-gated Lincoln content was accessed or represented as extracted.

133
00:09:02.030 --> 00:09:08.585
The public CDIAC Elect>Ed outline contributed broad themes of strategic planning and long-term impact.

134
00:09:08.585 --> 00:09:10.770
No gated Elect>Ed lesson was used.

135
00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:26.773
The life-cycle, capital-planning, expenditure, asset, and performance concepts adapt general ideas from Municipal Finances: A Handbook for Local Governments, edited by Catherine Farvacque-Vitkovic and Mihaly Kopanyi, World Bank, 2014, CC BY 3.0 IGO.

136
00:09:26.773 --> 00:09:36.992
Changes include an original Torrance facility scenario, multi-criteria scoring, confidence ratings, break-even analysis, distribution review, and a pre-mortem.

137
00:09:36.992 --> 00:09:40.077
The World Bank does not endorse this adaptation.

138
00:09:40.077 --> 00:09:52.866
Additional grounding comes from Institute for Local Government financial-management resources and City of Torrance public budgets, CIPs, project dashboards, agenda reports, budget reviews, and ACFRs.

139
00:09:52.866 --> 00:09:55.694
Good comparison does not eliminate judgment.

140
00:09:55.694 --> 00:10:03.020
It disciplines judgment by making needs, assumptions, tradeoffs, risk, and public values visible on the same page.

141
00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:06.720
Next, we examine municipal debt and long-term commitments.
